00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:16.240
I'm going to walk you
through, with the help of some

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.910
of my coworkers,
five major launches

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.700
today covering the
gamut of what you

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.510
need to see in a solution
like Google Drive.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.970
What those five
launches sum up to,

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.610
is that today's
the day where Drive

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.860
is really ready for
large enterprises

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.550
and we're very excited to be
able to take you through it.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.902
Before we get into
the details, I

00:01:39.902 --> 00:01:41.860
want to call out an
elephant in the room, which

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.310
is all of us come to
sessions like this

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.270
because we are really
excited about the possibility

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:53.080
of new digital technologies to
change the way that we work.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.530
You walk around this
conference and you

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.040
hear all about machine learning
and Kubernetes and Cloud

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.410
and the kinds of things
that we're doing in G Suite.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:08.919
The elephant in the room
is that as an IT leader,

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:12.850
you're really confronted
with an unfortunate choice.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:17.290
On the one hand, you can adopt
revolutionary technologies that

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:21.750
really make amazing improvements
available to your business

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:28.510
and suffer the risks that
come along with those.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:32.210
Teams come to work the
day after some big tech

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.500
rollout and the help desk
phone is ringing off the hook.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.150
People aren't able to get
access to their content

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.630
and business grinds
to a halt. You

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.090
have to decide whether
you want the revolution

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.360
and the improvement
with the attendant risks

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.120
or same old, same old.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.400
Predictability, stability,
comfort, and control.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.350
Those are both
important things and you

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.460
shouldn't have to choose.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.850
In reality, your business
needs both of these things.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.670
Your competitors are
not standing idly by.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.530
Your competitors are innovating.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.590
They're changing
and you need to make

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.780
the same kind of
revolutionary changes

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.720
or you risk being left behind.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.430
But on the other hand, if
you're a large enterprise--

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.160
tens or even 100,000 employees--

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.400
predictability is critical
and you shouldn't have

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.100
to choose between these things.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:31.040
Now, the perception of
Google, and rightly so,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.530
is that Google,
historically, has

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:37.690
been very focused on the
left-hand side of this visual.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.450
And it's for good reason.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.750
Google Search.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.780
Who remembers using AltaVista?

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.220
OK, I see a lot of hands.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.760
The first time you used Google
Search, your eyes popped open

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.620
and like oh, there's no
comparison between these two

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:51.190
things.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.350
When I got my first
Gmail account,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.170
the email that I had been
using with Hotmail at the time

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.540
had two megabytes of storage
and Google rolled out

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.031
a gigabyte of storage
with Gmail on day one.

00:04:01.031 --> 00:04:03.280
These are the kind of things
that Google is known for.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.300
It's in our DNA.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.540
The perception is this is
where we really focus, perhaps

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.880
to the exclusion of the
other side of that visual.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.620
Drive is no exception here.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.870
I'll through a couple of things.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.529
Google Drive, since day one,
has supported a maximum file

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:22.420
size of five terabytes.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.620
That's 1000 times
the maximum file

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.320
size of our primary competitor.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.740
We were the first to
launch unlimited quota.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.290
Security has been
built in from day one.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.840
All the content you
upload to Google Drive

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.240
gets automatically
scanned for viruses,

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.850
and that's been from day one.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.710
I'll give you a really
impressive visual

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.840
here of something that
launched last year.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.110
You can now use natural language
English queries to search

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:51.370
for your content in Drive.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.640
So when I want to find
something from my coworker Ron,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.780
I can say spreadsheets
from January with Ron.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.870
And it parses my English
grammar and returns what I need.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.980
One final example and
then I'll move on.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:09.900
100% of the content you
upload to Google Drive

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.450
gets run through our optical
character recognition system.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.230
So it could be a PDF,
of essentially image

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.560
files, that are coming in
from your legal department.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.360
They're all getting
scanned and when

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.830
you need to go
back and figure out

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.530
what your indemnity clauses
were in some contract,

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.990
you type in the word
indemnity into Google Drive

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.660
and all of those documents
get returned to you,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.420
even though they weren't
actually written in free text.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.230
So Drive, Google--
we've historically

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:41.650
focused on the left-hand
side of that equation.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.130
But we really
understand and have

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.650
gone through the cultural
and organizational

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:53.160
pivot to deliver enterprises
the controls and the guardrails

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.950
that they need to be able
to access that innovation.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.860
Because we know, as a
responsible IT leader,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.700
you can't just go do the next
hottest thing because buildings

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:05.450
could fall down.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.991
Are you going to be able to meet
your compliance obligations?

00:06:07.991 --> 00:06:09.410
Are you putting
your organization

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.340
at risk for a data breach?

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.670
Are you being the responsible
IT leader you need to be?

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.070
So we're changing the story.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.290
The five launches we're
walking through today

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.960
really focus on the right-hand
side of that equation,

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.680
so that you don't have to
make this either/or choice.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.140
We're letting you get
both of these together.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.200
Google will always invest
on the left-hand side

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.340
in the innovation.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:35.120
We have significantly grown the
size of the Google Drive team

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.340
over the course of
the last three years.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:40.190
Spent hundreds of
hours in the field

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:42.050
and hundreds of
engineering years

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.510
developing the
capabilities that you

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:49.430
need around control,
security, compliance,

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.160
to deliver that reliable
solution you need.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.980
So there are four fundamental
challenges the launches

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.314
we're rolling out today address.

00:06:56.314 --> 00:06:58.854
I'm going to walk you through
each of those at a high level--

00:06:58.854 --> 00:07:01.040
bring up some of my
coworkers to talk you

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.320
through the details
of those capabilities.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.480
We're going to do
a few demos and I

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.110
think you'll see that these
launches that roll out today

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:12.620
really make Drive the enterprise
capability that you need.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.470
So challenge number one,
that we need to address,

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:21.914
is all of your employees have
a set of expectations and needs

00:07:21.914 --> 00:07:24.080
that have evolved over the
course of, at this point,

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.710
almost 30 years, for how they
find and work with content.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:30.800
And those expectations have been
built up from how they interact

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.360
with network file shares and
on-prem content management

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:36.500
systems--

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.450
things like SharePoint.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:43.030
All of the file sync and share
solutions on the market today

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:46.880
are a fundamental mismatch
versus these user expectations.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.990
They were designed, initially,
as a way for individuals

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:52.790
to store their
content in the cloud

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.340
and sync it across
multiple devices.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.680
They weren't designed
with a team in mind.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.700
And teams have these different
expectations and patterns

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:02.310
of working.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:05.570
So we have to design a
storage content management

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:08.330
system that works for
the way that teams work.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:09.830
So that's point number one.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.290
Think about the end
users in your enterprise.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:15.650
Now, if we can take care of the
end users in your enterprise,

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.680
as an IT admin you start
to think OK, maybe this

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.080
is something my people
would want to use,

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.450
but is it going to work for me?

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:25.850
Is this going to allow me
to fulfill the compliance

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.390
obligations that I have to
protect against data breaches--

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:34.340
to control how our confidential
information gets shared, when

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.110
and with whom, and for how long.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:40.130
So we need to take care of your
needs and we're doing that.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:43.510
If we can address that problem,
you start to get excited.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.320
OK, my users and
my organization are

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:46.970
going to get what they need.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.220
I'm going to get the
control that I need.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:51.630
It would be great if we
were on this solution,

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:57.740
but the prospect of migrating
from the existing on-prem

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:01.070
storage that you have into
this new solution in the cloud

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:02.880
can just seem daunting.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.200
To quote one of my coworkers,
his favorite expression

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:10.490
is let's think about whether
the juice is worth the squeeze.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.980
And in this case,
unless we help you

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.819
with migration
capabilities, the juice

00:09:14.819 --> 00:09:16.610
might not be worth the
squeeze-- could just

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:20.240
be too high of a barrier
and too disruptive.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.630
Imagine people
come in on day one

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:25.040
and the content's all
in a different place

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.410
or the permissions
aren't mapped properly.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:31.520
Or it could just be that there
were so many years and decades

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.910
of content built up in
these preexisting systems,

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:37.800
that when people come into
the new system all of a sudden

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.700
they feel like they're under
a deluge of old, irrelevant

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.080
content and they can't find
what they're looking for.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.170
So we're investing in
migration capabilities

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.060
that solve this problem.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:47.720
Point four.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.060
If we can take care
of your end users--

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.730
if we can take care
of you and IT--

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:55.040
and then we can
help you migrate--

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:57.200
your next worry--
you're not done yet--

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:59.960
your next worry is OK I
put all my data there,

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.460
what's going to break?

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:03.170
All these tools,
all these business

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:05.030
processes that we've
architected that

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.040
assume the content's in
this particular place

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.050
and it's accessed
in a certain way--

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:10.420
are those going to stop working?

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:13.460
Is my finance team going
to be able to execute

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.860
their end of quarter
close process using

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.860
all those interlinked
Excel spreadsheets?

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.830
Is that going to work?

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.670
So we have a launch rolling
out today that specifically

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:25.160
addresses the backwards
compatibility needs that you

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:30.290
have that make Drive work with
all of those existing business

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:31.670
processes and tools.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.290
The result is Drive's stronger.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:34.576
Drive's enterprise-grade.

00:10:34.576 --> 00:10:36.450
You don't have to make
this either/or choice.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.390
You can have it both ways.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:40.910
So point number one, legacy
fileshares and content

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:43.040
management expectations.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.920
This is a very simple model from
our user experience research

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:49.880
team of how users think about
what content goes where.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.370
Most users are still
in a mode of thinking,

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:55.160
my stuff goes on my hard
drive, my team's stuff goes

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:57.470
on the server, whether that
server's a network-mapped

00:10:57.470 --> 00:10:58.460
fileshare--

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:01.370
whether that server is a
content management system.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:05.630
Unfortunately, this isn't how
existing cloud storage systems

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:06.860
are architected.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.140
So get ready to raise your hand.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.990
How many of you,
in the last week,

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.900
have instant messaged one of
your coworkers and said hey,

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:20.840
I can't find whatever,
can you send me the link?

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.100
Right, a lot of hands go up.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.990
That's a representation
of the fact

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.780
that existing tools
aren't working.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.720
Teams have a really hard
time finding their content,

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.760
discovering it,
getting back to it,

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.250
organizing it, understanding
who has access.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:40.700
So today we're announcing
the general availability

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:44.495
of a new capability
called Team Drives.

00:11:44.495 --> 00:11:46.740
Of everything I've
done in my career,

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.700
this is one of the two largest
projects I've ever worked on.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:54.140
It is essentially a fundamental
rewrite of all of Google Drive,

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.510
from just under the
user interface layer

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.490
all the way down to
the storage system,

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:01.310
including the API,
all of the clients,

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.260
everything else in between.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:04.670
It changes the sharing model.

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:06.530
It changes the
ownership model to make

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:08.030
it appropriate for teams.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:11.170
My coworker, Diane Chaleff,
is going to come up on stage,

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:13.370
walk you through a
bunch of this in detail,

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:15.240
and then give you a great demo.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:22.060
DIANE CHALEFF: OK, so I
know most you don't know me,

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.990
but I really like to cheer.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.630
It's not a joke, so we're
going to all get excited.

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:27.840
Team Drives, woohoo!

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.340
Team Drive, yeah!

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:31.540
Awesome.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:34.750
So we're going to stay on the
slides for a quick minute.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:36.610
So bear with me.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.530
I'm going to run through
a few slides quickly

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.780
to talk about what
Team Drives is,

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:43.310
how it can be helpful
for your organization,

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:46.010
and then we'll jump
into some demo.

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:50.260
So first up, the biggest change
in Team Drives that we bring

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:54.070
is that we make Drive
transparent, predictable,

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.920
and understandable
for large teams.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:00.400
And we do this primarily by
introducing a new sharing model

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.040
that has one very basic rule.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:06.700
Everyone on the Team Drive
sees everything inside.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.910
Your users will no longer
question, oh well when

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:13.090
I added John to the
Team Drive, did he

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:15.610
only see half the files
or some of the files?

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:17.770
No, it's super straightforward.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:19.750
Everyone sees everything inside.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:22.540
Likewise, when I put a
file into the Team Drive,

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.850
I know exactly who has access.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:29.920
So users don't under-share
or over-share their content.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.170
So next up here
is that we ensure

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:34.360
that new team
members automatically

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.490
get content right away.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:38.860
And when we do this,
it's because when

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:41.650
you're added to a Team Drive,
that Team Drive automatically

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:43.610
appears in your navigation.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:45.970
So how is a user
added to Team Drive?

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:47.410
Well one of two ways.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:51.580
Team Drives are composed of
individuals, like you and me,

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:55.120
and/or groups, like
marketing at my domain.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.640
So you can think about this
as the to line on an email

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:58.820
address.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:01.240
So once someone's
added to a Team Drive,

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.370
we automatically
put that Team Drive

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:05.020
in their left-hand navigation.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.960
And this is really helpful when
it's an employee's first day

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:08.800
of work.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:11.650
Often, you're provisioning
them to many systems,

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.890
perhaps using Google Group
or Active Directory to do so,

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:17.590
and now that content will
seamlessly flow through.

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:21.680
And they'll open Drive and
see all their team's work.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:25.580
So, most importantly, files
they put in the Team Drive--

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:28.370
I never want to have to wonder
like oh, it's on someone else's

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:29.400
local hard drive.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.030
And now they're on vacation
and I can't get access

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.280
or they've left the
team and moved on.

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:38.450
All files on the Team Drive
always belong to the Team Drive

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:41.390
and will permanently be there
even as team members change

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:44.280
over time.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:48.560
And lastly here, permissions are
easy to understand and manage.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:50.750
So we have four
different user roles

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.991
that you can have for your
users on the Team Drive.

00:14:53.991 --> 00:14:56.240
And I'm going to take a quick
second to talk about one

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:59.180
of those roles,
which is edit access.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.059
And we think this role
is really special,

00:15:01.059 --> 00:15:02.600
because it's
essentially the role you

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.750
can give your users so they
can collaborate, but not break

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:07.140
anything.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.940
So users with edit access
can add new content,

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:12.890
can edit existing
content, which I

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:15.860
like to refer to as happy
collaborative actions,

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:19.010
but they can't delete
content or move content out

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:20.150
of the Team Drive.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.040
And so this means if
you have a user who

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.440
is a little hesitant to
adopt new technology,

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:27.680
you can say to them, guess what,
you literally can't do anything

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:30.850
wrong besides upload like,
thousands of photos of puppies,

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:34.130
which who does not want
to see photos of puppies?

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:35.990
That usually goes
over a lot better,

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:37.780
like, puppies are really fun.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.300
Puppies are really fun.

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:43.420
OK, so I know I talked
a lot about the user.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:45.530
I want to talk for a
minute about the admin.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.720
We think it's really
important in Drive,

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:50.270
to both be giving
users capabilities

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.190
to do the actions
that they need,

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:54.530
as well as giving
admins the capability

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:56.690
to understand what's
going on with the content

00:15:56.690 --> 00:15:58.380
in their organization.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:00.380
So we have a whole
array of admin features.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:03.030
I'm only going to touch on
a couple of them quickly.

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.820
So one is admins can see
a list of all the Team

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:09.440
Drives on their domain and they
can manage those Team Drives.

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:12.400
And that management
comes in a few flavors.

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.400
So first off, users are
creating Team Drives

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:16.720
and editing membership.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:25.270
And admins can also be
editing membership for a Team

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:26.530
Drive behind the scenes.

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:30.430
So admins can be adding users,
removing users, and changing

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:32.590
those users' roles.

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.350
Also, with Team
Drives, all your events

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:37.090
show up the Drive
Audit log, just

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:39.100
like files do today
and actions today that

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:40.750
take place in my Drive.

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:42.670
In addition to
these capabilities,

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:45.610
we have the capabilities
to restore deleted content.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:48.250
So if you have a user that
deletes content and wants

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:49.900
to get it back,
you, as an admin,

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.110
can go in and recover
also other Team Drives

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:57.980
and also find Team Drives
with specific sets of members.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:01.400
So we know that Team Drives
is a really big step forward

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:03.860
in how Drive might be
used in your organization.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.545
And so we want to aid in
that deployment process.

00:17:06.545 --> 00:17:08.790
And we do that in four ways.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:11.990
So, first off, Team Drive,
it's in your control

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:13.369
how you deploy it.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:17.750
You can pick which users in your
company can create Team Drives.

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:19.940
And generally the
way we see this used

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:24.210
is that IT admins or team leads
will go in and make a few Team

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.450
Drives for their team
to get them started--

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:28.109
maybe have a lunch and learn--

00:17:28.109 --> 00:17:29.990
get everyone familiar
with Team Drives

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.829
and then turn on creation for
the department or the team.

00:17:32.829 --> 00:17:34.370
So we're really
excited that you have

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:36.280
a lot of control over
that deployment process

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.960
and encourage you to
roll out Team Drives

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.310
as it makes sense for
your organization.

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.970
Next up, we also offer a
change management guide, again,

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.280
to help with some of
these best practices.

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:48.320
In-product training
that walks your users

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.680
through the new feature and,
as always, our Help Center.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:54.590
So, with that, I'm going
to jump over to the demo.

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:57.175
And I'm about to get a lot
taller, so I'm really excited.

00:17:57.175 --> 00:17:58.150
You guys ready?

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:06.320
OK, so I know on the stage
that I was working at Google,

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:08.390
but now I work for
a toy manufacturer.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:11.090
So just picture I changed jobs.

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:13.040
And I'm going to
walk you through what

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:15.140
my life is like as a
toy manufacturer using

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:16.880
Drive and Team Drives.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:20.690
So when I first
joined my company

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:24.200
I got told that I was going to
be on the remote control cars

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:25.220
team.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:27.170
And I immediately was
like, ugh, how am I

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:29.360
going to get up to speed
on everything that we've

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:32.210
been doing with making
remote control cars?

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:35.300
Well, luckily, my team had
already been using Team Drives.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:40.630
So on day one of work, I went
in, I saw all of my Team Drives

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:45.510
there, and all the files that
belong to the remote control

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:47.010
cars team.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:48.257
So, pretty straight forward.

00:18:48.257 --> 00:18:50.840
I actually did a really good job
working on the remote control

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:52.210
car team.

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:53.460
I'm pretty excited about that.

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:55.181
So my boss came by
and said, you know

00:18:55.181 --> 00:18:57.680
what, we're going to put you
in charge of the new Wi-Fi toys

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.610
team.

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:01.220
It's toys and it's
Wi-Fi coming together.

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:02.282
So this is very exciting.

00:19:02.282 --> 00:19:02.990
You guys excited?

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:04.340
Wi-Fi toys.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.461
Yeah, maybe?

00:19:06.461 --> 00:19:06.960
We tried.

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:09.090
We didn't know if that
was going to go over well.

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:12.710
So I got the team together
and we started brainstorming

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:14.210
and had a few ideas.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:15.800
And I have all these
files here, but I

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:18.590
want to make sure that
they're available to my team.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:22.430
And so I'm going to go
ahead and make a Team Drive.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.200
We'll jump back in.

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.600
I'm going to make a new Team
Drive called Wi-Fi toys.

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:34.430
And, provided the
internet's working--

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:34.930
great.

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:37.655
So I now have the
Wi-Fi toys Team Drive.

00:19:37.655 --> 00:19:39.780
But right now this Team
Drive doesn't look so good.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:42.010
There is no members
on it, besides me,

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:43.690
and there's no files inside.

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:45.784
So let's go take care
of those two problems.

00:19:45.784 --> 00:19:47.200
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.120
is I'm going to go
back to my Drive,

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:52.960
I'm going to grab the files
that are relevant here,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.880
and I'm going to move
them into my Team Drive.

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:57.250
And if you watch in
the lower left corner,

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:59.380
it's going to tell me
that all the files moved

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:00.910
and that everyone
who is an existing

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:04.660
collaborator on this Team
Drive now has access.

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.360
But as we know, there's
no other people right now

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:08.770
on my Team Drive.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:10.060
So let's go add some members.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:13.810
I'm going to add
two people here.

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.060
I'm going to add a
group, Wi-Fi toys.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:22.180
And I'm going to add
my summer intern, Clay.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:24.055
And I'll leave them
a quick message.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.270
And send.

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:34.890
And now they have access.

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:37.590
And we can see that I've
updated from one member to three

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:39.520
members instantly.

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:41.840
So Clay is a summer
intern and this

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:43.820
is his first job he's ever had.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:46.880
He's really excited to be on
the team, but as his manager,

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:47.790
I'm a little nervous.

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.030
I want to let him
contribute to the space,

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:52.790
but I don't necessarily want
him deleting stuff or adding

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:54.270
other people in the company.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:56.330
So I'm going to go and
change Clay's role.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:58.280
And so all of the
options for Team Drives

00:20:58.280 --> 00:20:59.800
are under the main menu.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:01.700
I'll go to manage members, here.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:03.920
I'm going to take Clay and
I'm going to move him down

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:05.210
to edit access.

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:08.120
Again, that role, so he can
be an effective collaborator,

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:11.160
but not hurt our space.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:12.870
Now, the last thing
that's come up

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.630
is Arie, who's my
colleague in finance.

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.424
He is really all
about the numbers

00:21:17.424 --> 00:21:18.840
and how much money
we're spending.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:20.965
He doesn't care at all
about the toys we're making,

00:21:20.965 --> 00:21:22.860
which is a little
upsetting, but I get it.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:24.690
He just wants to know
about the budget.

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.510
And so he doesn't want to
be added to this Team Drive.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:29.880
He wants the one
file that he needs.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:38.370
So I'm going to
take our budget file

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:41.430
and I'm going to
share it with Arie.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:46.390
So Arie's been added.

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:49.240
So now Arie will have access
to this one very important file

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:51.100
in our Team Drive.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:22:03.630
And if I go back
and I look, we can

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.510
see that it's our three
members of our team

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:09.450
with their respective
roles plus Arie.

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.662
So that's my quick demo
today for Team Drives.

00:22:11.662 --> 00:22:13.620
And now I'm going to hand
it back over to Alex.

00:22:17.394 --> 00:22:18.560
ALEX VOGENTHALER: Thank you.

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:24.930
OK, so as I
mentioned, Team Drives

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:28.080
has been an early
adopter program.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.260
We announced that
back in August.

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:32.640
And I'll let everyone read
this quote for a second--

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.590
I'm not going to read
it out loud to you--

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:37.380
from Troy at Whirlpool
about how this

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:38.976
is helping their organization.

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:46.740
This is the sentiment
that we get from everyone

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.900
who's tried this feature out.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:52.560
It's been testing with hundreds
of thousands of customers.

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:55.040
At this point we've put over--

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:56.840
I was estimating
it this morning--

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:00.540
over 1,000 hours from our
user experience research team

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:01.380
into this.

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:05.880
And, as you can see, when
Diane walked through that demo,

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:10.560
Team Drives strikes a
really great balance.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:14.340
We really think we found the
sweet spot between the controls

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.170
and the capabilities
and the sophistication

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:19.760
that people need while also
being a very easy to use

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:20.350
product.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:23.457
So there's some more
information on the last slide

00:23:23.457 --> 00:23:25.290
of the presentation
about how you can enable

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:26.910
Team Drives for your domain.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:28.500
I'll underscore the
point that Diane

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:30.030
made, that you're in control.

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:32.250
You can choose to
turn on Team Drives

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:33.870
today for your entire domain.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:35.820
Let 1,000 flowers bloom.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:37.860
Let people there start
creating Team Drives

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.750
or you can take a
cautious approach.

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:42.210
Plan out what you want the
information, organization,

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:43.890
of your domain to be.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:47.400
And only then roll it out
to everyone in your domain.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:50.160
So if you remember those four
boxes we had on the screen,

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:51.960
we've dealt with the first.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:55.380
Users who work in
teams have expectations

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:58.980
about what team ownership means
versus individual ownership.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:00.360
Where my stuff goes.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:02.400
Where my team stuff goes.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.500
And that's what Team
Drives addresses.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:07.350
What we have to do now,
though, is make you, the admin,

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:11.010
confident in moving your data
from your on-prem, legacy

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:12.330
system into Drive.

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:16.590
And you, by rights,
should be concerned.

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:17.700
You should be nervous.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:20.430
You should have us
prove to you that Drive

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:23.936
is going to be an environment
that will keep your data safe,

00:24:23.936 --> 00:24:25.560
reduce the likelihood
of a data breach,

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:28.530
and allow you to fulfill
your compliance obligations.

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:31.650
Before I get into
the major launch

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.510
that we're rolling
out today, I just want

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:36.510
to highlight this is not a
one and done effort for us.

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:40.110
We actually now have multiple
dedicated teams focused

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:42.810
on building admin controls.

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:46.410
And there's a steady drumbeat
of launches in this area.

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:49.410
We had a major
launch in this area

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:51.029
just as recently as January.

00:24:51.029 --> 00:24:53.070
Here we are, two months
later, with the next one.

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:54.486
So I'll get into
that in a second.

00:24:54.486 --> 00:24:57.090
But, a recap of what
we launched in January.

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:58.824
So, data breaches.

00:24:58.824 --> 00:25:00.990
You should prevent data
breaches in the first place,

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:03.360
rather than having great
tools to remediate them.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.745
You should have
both, but prevention

00:25:05.745 --> 00:25:06.870
is where you want to start.

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.900
So Drive Data Loss
Prevention allows

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:15.030
you to identify critical
content that shouldn't be shared

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:16.380
outside of your organization.

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:20.190
The classic examples here
are the human resources team

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:22.710
that has files full of
employees' social security

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:27.600
numbers or maybe an accounts
receivable team or order

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:31.290
management team that
has credit card numbers.

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:36.390
You need to ensure that those
numbers, those key identifiers,

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:40.110
don't inadvertently or
purposefully get shared

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:41.740
outside of your organization.

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:44.040
And it's not enough
to just get reporting

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.680
after the fact about when that
happens because then the cat's

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:47.700
already out of the bag.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:49.500
You need to be able
to scan, identify,

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:52.980
and proactively prevent
that content from leaking.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:55.620
And that's what Drive
Data Loss Prevention does.

00:25:55.620 --> 00:26:00.330
It supports a myriad of
special numbers and patterns

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:01.450
across the world.

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:02.580
This is just a subset--

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.950
what we could get on the slide.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.740
Social Security numbers
in the United States.

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.660
SWIFT banking account numbers.

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:11.160
The list goes on and on.

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:16.620
But you're not just limited to a
predetermined set of templates.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.000
This is fully customizable.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:21.210
Whatever industry you're in,
whatever geography you're in,

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.310
you can come in and
create the rules that

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:25.412
will prevent that
content from leaking

00:26:25.412 --> 00:26:26.620
outside of your organization.

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:28.320
You can customize it.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:31.230
One of the issues
that many solutions

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:32.970
on the market like
this suffer from

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:35.280
is the boy who cried
wolf syndrome, where

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:38.640
they're constantly alerting
and you just end up getting

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:40.000
used to ignoring the alert.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:42.120
So there was one
little thing over here,

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:43.661
there was one little
thing over here,

00:26:43.661 --> 00:26:46.110
and then you don't miss
there were 10,000 hits.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.210
And that slips
past because you've

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:51.520
trained yourself to not pay
attention to these warnings.

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:54.810
There are content count
and frequency thresholds

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:59.430
in Data Loss Prevention so that
you can go deal with an issue

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:01.396
when it rises to
a certain level.

00:27:01.396 --> 00:27:04.020
If you remember that slide I had
in the beginning about Drive's

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:06.420
innovations, one of those
fundamental innovations

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:09.810
is optical character recognition
for every piece of content

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:11.460
that gets uploaded
to Drive, including

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:13.920
all the scanned documents
that might come in from legal

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:15.840
or an orders team.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:18.210
We OCR all that
content, so that content

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:21.060
is also protected by DLP.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:23.160
So that was in January.

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:28.200
That's available to everyone
on the G Suite Enterprise SKU.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:31.550
The launch today is in the
area of content governance.

00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:33.900
I've talked a lot about
compliance so far.

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:36.150
If you're a large
company, you have

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.410
a lot of compliance burdens.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:40.350
You are probably subject
to some rules that

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:44.910
say data from the
finance team all

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:47.910
has to be retained for a
minimum of seven years.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:51.030
And you, as the IT
admin, in conjunction

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:52.650
with your legal
team, want to ensure

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.780
that 100% of that
content gets deleted

00:27:54.780 --> 00:27:57.059
at seven years and a day.

00:27:57.059 --> 00:27:58.600
You need tools to
be able to do that.

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:01.060
Another use case is if
you're a big company,

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:04.570
you probably get sued
pretty regularly.

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:06.970
In a lawsuit, you
need to make sure

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:08.470
that no documents are destroyed.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.678
So you have to be able to
put people on a legal hold.

00:28:10.678 --> 00:28:13.150
And not just extract the
content that they have,

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:15.640
but guarantee that any
matching content can't

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:18.080
get deleted from the system.

00:28:18.080 --> 00:28:19.570
This is what Vault
for Drive does.

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:22.060
It's in general
availability today.

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:24.400
It lets you set these
retention and deletion

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:26.950
controls for all the content
that you have stored in Drive.

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:28.490
It's very easy to set up.

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:31.570
You can set up rules based on
what organization someone works

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:33.080
in, for example, finance.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:36.820
You can set up rules so
anything related to Project X--

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.160
maybe there's a lawsuit
related to Project X--

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:40.840
it's all going to get retained.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:43.150
And a really important point--

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:46.270
when we say that's deleted,
it's really deleted.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:51.910
We are subject to a number
of compliance certifications

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:55.630
that require us to be
audited in such a way

00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:59.560
that we can guarantee
contractually that when

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:02.050
your data is deleted,
whether by you, your users,

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:04.180
or automatically by
the Vault system,

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.347
it's really off our servers.

00:29:05.347 --> 00:29:07.305
You don't have to worry
that that data is going

00:29:07.305 --> 00:29:09.250
to somehow come back to
life somewhere, which

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:13.210
has been an issue with
some solutions in the past.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:14.860
So that's Vault.

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.750
If you think about
DLP and Vault,

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:18.190
those are just two examples.

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:19.450
Vault's launching today.

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:21.040
There are many
things we've launched

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:22.810
and many more that
are coming up.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:26.350
Expiring sharing is
one recent launch.

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:28.780
We're really investing
in this whole area

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:29.860
of controls for admin.

00:29:29.860 --> 00:29:32.290
So you, as the admin,
can now get comfortable

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:36.686
that if you move your
content into Drive,

00:29:36.686 --> 00:29:38.310
you're taking care
of your obligations.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:41.707
So you could imagine,
hey, this is a solution

00:29:41.707 --> 00:29:42.790
I might want to get on to.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:46.240
But the idea of migrating
on to one of these solutions

00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:48.820
can be pretty daunting.

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:52.360
Not only do you actually have
to move file contents themselves

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:55.600
and extract them out of a legacy
content management system,

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.180
you also have to map all
of the permissions properly

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:00.134
to make sure no one
loses access to content

00:30:00.134 --> 00:30:02.050
and also so that content
doesn't get opened up

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:04.510
too broadly, inadvertently.

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:09.410
You also need to deal with
the issue of legacy data.

00:30:09.410 --> 00:30:11.920
You don't want users
in this new system

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:14.800
to feel like they're
drowning under data

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:18.070
that's accumulated over years
in your preexisting system.

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:20.290
So migration is something
we need to help you with.

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:22.750
And so we're very
pleased to announce

00:30:22.750 --> 00:30:25.240
our acquisition of AppBridge.

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:29.710
AppBridge is the number one
Google partner for migration

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:30.760
onto the G Suite.

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:33.700
They have an enterprise-grade
solution, a background

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:36.370
in migration from
companies like Metalogix,

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:41.030
and the product they've built
is really quite impressive.

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:44.260
So I'm pleased to bring Cameron
