00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:11.910
So how can the technology scale
to do something like this?

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:14.497
How could it achieve a
project result like that?

00:35:14.497 --> 00:35:16.080
What you're seeing
on the screen here,

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:19.290
right now, is a very
high-level X-ray

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:22.320
of a single transformation
server in an AppBridge

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:23.730
topology.

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:24.860
This might be one of many.

00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:25.860
You might have hundreds.

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:29.430
If you're on GCP, you
might have thousands.

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:32.280
And really what this
diagram portrays

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:35.310
is that even at the lowest
scale, a single server,

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:40.830
the technology is designed
to automatically parallelize

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:44.080
the work that needs to
be done on that server.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:46.500
So traditional problems
you may have encountered--

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:49.470
multi-threading,
centralizing your logging,

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:52.396
optimizing your
Google API calls--

00:35:52.396 --> 00:35:54.270
those are all problems
that this architecture

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:55.930
is designed to solve for you.

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:01.240
So let's go a little
higher in the process

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:03.760
and talk a little bit about
migration configuration

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:08.050
and how the experience of
using this solution is.

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:09.850
What you see on the
screen in front of you

00:36:09.850 --> 00:36:12.580
is a snapshot of
the Bridges page.

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:14.980
So a Bridge is the term
that we at AppBridge

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:18.670
use to refer to a
migration template.

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:21.730
And so if you are
configuring a migration,

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:25.210
you want to say come from
SharePoint to G Suite.

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:26.560
You set up your mappings.

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:28.370
You set up how your
permissions transform

00:36:28.370 --> 00:36:29.590
during that migration.

00:36:29.590 --> 00:36:31.180
You set up the
filters on what data

00:36:31.180 --> 00:36:33.040
you'd like to leave
behind because it hasn't

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:35.470
been accessed in 10 years.

00:36:35.470 --> 00:36:39.580
That's all done and configured
right here, in this Bridge.

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:42.070
But one of the really cool
things about this architecture

00:36:42.070 --> 00:36:44.860
is when you run that
Bridge, under the hood,

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:46.810
it will take your
migration corpus

00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:50.050
and it will split it up into
what we call Partitions.

00:36:50.050 --> 00:36:51.580
And it will do
this intelligently,

00:36:51.580 --> 00:36:54.100
as you can see on the
right-hand side there.

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:56.020
These Partitions
will automatically

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:59.110
be load-balanced across your
distributed topology, which

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:01.090
means they get run
on multiple servers.

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:04.450
The more you have, great,
the more you can run.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:06.070
And in Google Cloud
Platform, this

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:09.750
allows unprecedented
scalability for migrations.

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:12.080
The goal and result
of this technology

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:15.630
is it will create an
optimization based off

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:18.510
of your source corpus,
your actual data, that

00:37:18.510 --> 00:37:24.390
is very intelligent
and by doing so,

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:27.382
it helps to remove the
onus on your migration team

00:37:27.382 --> 00:37:28.590
from configuring it that way.

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:35.670
So, as I said before, we do
meet you where you are today.

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:37.470
If you are in the
Cloud already--

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:42.300
using a technology like Office
365 or Dropbox or Box, Ignite,

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:43.410
et cetera--

00:37:43.410 --> 00:37:45.810
or if you're on
premises and maybe even

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:48.650
using an older version of
one of these technologies--

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:49.860
MOSS 2007.

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:53.670
If you know the acronym,
you're probably using it--

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:56.850
Exchange 2007, older
versions of SharePoint,

00:37:56.850 --> 00:37:58.740
older versions of Exchange--

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:02.310
our goal is really to
support you in your drive

00:38:02.310 --> 00:38:03.840
to the Google Cloud.

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:06.660
Even G Suite domains can be
used as a source for migration,

00:38:06.660 --> 00:38:10.470
which as it is on topic
for this presentation,

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:13.680
I'm very pleased to announce
that direct migration to Google

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:15.648
Team Drives is supported.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.550
So let's talk about
transformation.

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:29.610
It is very rare that,
in our experience,

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:31.530
that a migration is
simply picking up

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:35.290
your data that lives here
and putting it there.

00:38:35.290 --> 00:38:38.590
Generally, there are specific
transformation points

00:38:38.590 --> 00:38:40.270
in your migration
project that you'll

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:42.970
want to ensure the
software is aware of

00:38:42.970 --> 00:38:46.230
and can handle
automatically for you.

00:38:46.230 --> 00:38:49.020
To help facilitate this,
I have a few features here

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:50.790
that I'm going to talk
through that really

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:53.910
help you plan your migration and
make sure that it's successful.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:58.200
The first is the software
includes an entirely fleshed

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:02.730
out discovery, scanning, and
reporting functionality that

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:05.790
allows you to discover what your
corpus looks like before you

00:39:05.790 --> 00:39:07.530
actually run your migration.

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:09.540
This is things like
how many objects

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:11.320
do I have, not just
files and folders,

00:39:11.320 --> 00:39:12.361
but how many permissions?

00:39:12.361 --> 00:39:14.640
How many API calls am I
going to have to make?

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:16.140
If you've done a
migration, you know

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:18.300
that's probably the bottleneck.

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:20.070
Finding out that gives
a really good way

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:21.903
of finding out how long
your migrations will

00:39:21.903 --> 00:39:22.590
take in the end.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:25.724
Things like watch points--
how deep are my folders?

00:39:25.724 --> 00:39:27.390
Do I have too many
permissions in places

00:39:27.390 --> 00:39:29.160
where they shouldn't be?

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:30.600
This, then, leads
into the ability

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:33.180
to start your migrations
as soon as possible.

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:36.420
Incremental functionality
built into the solution

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:40.800
gives you the ability to run
your migrations initially

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:44.760
and then pick up anything
that's changed in later waves.

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:47.100
So good examples
of this are, say

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:49.740
I want to migrate all
my documents initially

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:51.210
to get the data
there for testing,

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:54.280
for getting the
migration out of the way,

00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:56.280
but then I want to migrate
my permissions later.

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:57.200
Incremental migration.

00:39:57.200 --> 00:39:58.830
Check a box, rerun it.

00:39:58.830 --> 00:40:00.300
No problem.

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:03.840
Another very common
case is if you

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.830
migrate a billion documents,
even with [? 6:9 ?] success,

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:08.580
you're going to have
a couple of failures.

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:12.980
So repairing errors during
migration is built in.

00:40:12.980 --> 00:40:15.770
You can pick up only the
failures and migrate those.

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:18.470
You don't have to
re-crawl the source, only

00:40:18.470 --> 00:40:21.950
re-migrate the failures, and now
if you fix them, they're good.

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:23.730
They're solved.

00:40:23.730 --> 00:40:27.210
And as somebody who has run
many migrations in my career,

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:30.030
the last point is really, really
important to me, personally.

00:40:30.030 --> 00:40:32.220
Validation.

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:34.830
To make sure that
our customers are

00:40:34.830 --> 00:40:38.010
able to be very confident in
the results of their migration,

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:40.950
we've built a data-centric
approach to our logs.

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:43.470
What I mean by that is
every single object--

00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:45.420
and I don't mean
document, I mean object--

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:48.660
in the migration is tracked
in your transaction log.

00:40:48.660 --> 00:40:51.120
What that means is every
permission, every file,

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:53.130
every folder, it's in there.

00:40:53.130 --> 00:40:55.530
You still get the summary
logs, the high-level throughput

00:40:55.530 --> 00:40:57.870
statistics, your
error categories,

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:00.150
to fix, broadly, what
might have gone wrong

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:02.730
if you have millions of
documents that have migrated.

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:06.480
But you also get the ability,
if a user files a support ticket

00:41:06.480 --> 00:41:10.020
post go live, to filter down
and find the specific document

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:11.660
and what happened to it.

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:13.020
Not only that, it's actionable.

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:16.260
So you can then fix the problem,
rerun that one document--

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:19.140
if you're a helpdesk employee
or someone else looking to solve

00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:21.139
this problem for the customer--

00:41:21.139 --> 00:41:21.930
right from the log.

00:41:26.360 --> 00:41:29.480
So I'm incredibly excited
about the future of migration

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:34.130
with the new partnership between
Google Cloud and AppBridge.

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:36.380
I want to close
with a quote that

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:39.230
