00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:54.320
Get you over the hump.

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:58.010
Number four is what happens
the day after migration?

00:42:58.010 --> 00:42:59.244
All the contents moved.

00:42:59.244 --> 00:43:00.410
We've dealt with the errors.

00:43:00.410 --> 00:43:02.300
You've validated the audit logs.

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.310
And now, if you're
Mitsui Home, you

00:43:04.310 --> 00:43:06.290
have tens of
terabytes of content

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:08.360
that used to be
within your walls,

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:12.170
that your users had access
to on the network fileshare,

00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:14.410
and now it's in the Cloud.

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:20.090
This laptop here probably has a
128 gig, SSD hard drive in it.

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:23.600
And many of your employees are
running on similar hardware.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:25.670
And you've now just put
10 terabytes of data

00:43:25.670 --> 00:43:26.850
into the cloud.

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:29.930
So how do you get your users
access to all that data

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:34.190
without breaking all of their
training, their expectations,

00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:35.270
and the workflows?

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:38.010
I mentioned the example
of a finance team

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.010
that's trying to execute
an end-of-quarter close

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:42.680
process with a bunch of
