00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:07.580
and give you a live demo.

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:16.080
AAKASH: All right.

00:44:16.080 --> 00:44:17.040
Thanks, Alex.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:17.640
Hi, everybody.

00:44:17.640 --> 00:44:18.660
So, I'm Aakash.

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:20.640
And I am just so
excited to introduce

00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:23.670
you to Drive File Stream today.

00:44:23.670 --> 00:44:26.040
Drive File Stream is a
brand new application,

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:28.710
built from scratch, for
accessing your Google Drive

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:31.320
files on a Mac or a PC.

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:33.210
It works seamlessly
with all of the tools

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:35.092
that your users use today.

00:44:35.092 --> 00:44:37.050
And all of your existing
workflows and business

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:38.355
processes can remain intact.

00:44:48.190 --> 00:44:50.110
So the magic all
starts by mounting

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:54.160
Google Drive as a network file
system on the user's computer.

00:44:54.160 --> 00:44:56.890
So you can see here, we've
got the G drive mounted next

00:44:56.890 --> 00:45:00.460
to the C drive, right
where a user would

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:03.640
expect to see their team files
if they were using a fileshare.

00:45:06.490 --> 00:45:07.910
And now because
drive file stream

00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:10.460
behaves like a
network file system,

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:13.760
the files aren't actually
stored on the user's disk.

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:15.710
Instead, they're
streamed on-demand when

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:18.119
the user goes to access them.

00:45:18.119 --> 00:45:20.660
And that's because we've heard
from customers again and again

00:45:20.660 --> 00:45:22.700
that when they use
traditional sync clients,

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:25.580
users will sign in and then
immediately their hard disks

00:45:25.580 --> 00:45:28.130
will explode because they fill
up their hard disks with all

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:31.220
the content that they
need from Google Drive.

00:45:31.220 --> 00:45:34.910
And so Drive File Stream makes
this a problem of the past.

00:45:34.910 --> 00:45:37.340
Users can access loads
and loads of files

00:45:37.340 --> 00:45:40.620
that occupy only a very small
footprint on their computer.

00:45:43.542 --> 00:45:45.000
In addition, we
wanted to make sure

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:47.820
that Drive File Stream was
really easy to understand

00:45:47.820 --> 00:45:49.290
for end users.

00:45:49.290 --> 00:45:53.244
And for users, there's
actually nothing new to learn.

00:45:53.244 --> 00:45:54.660
Drive File Stream
works seamlessly

00:45:54.660 --> 00:45:56.550
with all of the applications
they're used to.

00:45:56.550 --> 00:45:58.216
They can double click
on a Word document

00:45:58.216 --> 00:45:59.600
and it opens in Microsoft Word.

00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:03.470
Double click on a PSD and
it opens in Photoshop.

00:46:03.470 --> 00:46:05.730
Now, since the files are
being streamed on-demand, you

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:07.188
might be wondering,
hey, what do we

00:46:07.188 --> 00:46:08.970
do if we have some
teams that need access

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:10.560
to their files offline?

00:46:10.560 --> 00:46:12.900
So, for example, if they
frequently go on customer

00:46:12.900 --> 00:46:15.300
visits where the Wi-Fi is
shaky or they fly all the time

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:17.880
and they are on planes
and don't have Wi-Fi--

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:20.890
so we've made that really easy
in Drive File Stream as well.

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:22.620
So with Drive File
Stream users can

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:24.940
right-click on
any file or folder

00:46:24.940 --> 00:46:27.516
and choose make
available offline

00:46:27.516 --> 00:46:29.640
and then the files get
downloaded and kept in sync,

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:31.740
so that the user can access
them on their computer

00:46:31.740 --> 00:46:33.615
no matter where they
are, whenever they like.

00:46:36.100 --> 00:46:38.200
Next-- and this one is
really important to me--

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:39.964
Drive File Stream is fast.

00:46:39.964 --> 00:46:42.130
The team has worked really,
really hard to make sure

00:46:42.130 --> 00:46:43.990
that every action
that a user takes

00:46:43.990 --> 00:46:47.230
is super quick, whether it's
navigating through folders,

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:48.650
opening files--

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:51.580
just for a quick example,
the average first sync

00:46:51.580 --> 00:46:53.187
takes only about 23 seconds.

00:46:53.187 --> 00:46:55.270
So think about it, you
download Drive File Stream,

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:56.950
you install it, you
sign in, and then

00:46:56.950 --> 00:46:59.290
within seconds, you
can be up and running

00:46:59.290 --> 00:47:00.940
and ready to get productive.

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:03.130
When you compare this to
traditional sync tools,

00:47:03.130 --> 00:47:05.740
those can take sometimes
hours or even days

00:47:05.740 --> 00:47:08.364
before all of the content is
downloaded to the computer.

00:47:08.364 --> 00:47:10.030
So the difference
with Drive File Stream

00:47:10.030 --> 00:47:12.880
is because we're not making the
user wait for every single file

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:14.370
to get downloaded,
they can get up

00:47:14.370 --> 00:47:15.620
and running almost right away.

00:47:19.550 --> 00:47:24.340
And lastly, Drive File Stream
works great for end users.

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:26.180
So, that's good.

00:47:26.180 --> 00:47:28.650
But as an extra benefit,
IT professionals

00:47:28.650 --> 00:47:32.620
can rest easy knowing that
for the majority of files,

00:47:32.620 --> 00:47:34.530
the actual content
of the files is not

00:47:34.530 --> 00:47:36.400
sitting on the user's laptop.

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:39.210
So if a device were to get
lost or stolen, all of the data

00:47:39.210 --> 00:47:40.710
doesn't necessarily go with it.

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:43.320
So IT teams can be
comfortable knowing

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:46.699
that their important corporate
data is actually safe.

