00:51:54.670 --> 00:51:56.380
OK.

00:51:56.380 --> 00:51:58.690
So if you remember
the four points

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:01.060
that we started this
presentation talking about,

00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:03.820
we need a sharing,
organizational solution

00:52:03.820 --> 00:52:06.280
that works for your users--
lines up with the expectations

00:52:06.280 --> 00:52:07.960
that they have
coming from on-prem.

00:52:07.960 --> 00:52:10.150
We need to deal with
your issues, the admin.

00:52:10.150 --> 00:52:11.890
We have to solve migration.

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:14.380
And we have to make sure that
we don't break your business

00:52:14.380 --> 00:52:15.956
processes after you migrate.

00:52:15.956 --> 00:52:17.080
We've covered all of those.

00:52:17.080 --> 00:52:18.400
Four launches.

00:52:18.400 --> 00:52:20.260
Today, Team Drives.

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:21.640
DLP came in January.

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:22.630
Vault comes today.

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:26.560
The migration acquisition
with AppBridge and Drive File

00:52:26.560 --> 00:52:28.330
Stream today.

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:29.860
If there's one
thing you take away

00:52:29.860 --> 00:52:33.040
from this presentation,
what I want you to take away

00:52:33.040 --> 00:52:36.940
is the Drive team, in
particular, in G Suite overall,

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:39.790
are heavily invested
in the needs of IT

00:52:39.790 --> 00:52:42.190
leaders who are very cognizant
of the challenges you

00:52:42.190 --> 00:52:45.340
face when trying to migrate
on to a solution like that.

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:48.190
And we are addressing
these problems.

00:52:48.190 --> 00:52:51.760
If you remember that
left-right chart

00:52:51.760 --> 00:52:54.390
that I had up at the beginning--
the revolutionary innovations

00:52:54.390 --> 00:52:56.140
that come with the
chaos and the buildings

00:52:56.140 --> 00:53:00.460
that have fallen down
or predictability.

00:53:00.460 --> 00:53:02.140
Same old, same old--

00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:04.180
not really helping
your business innovate.

00:53:04.180 --> 00:53:05.770
We don't want you
to have to choose.

00:53:05.770 --> 00:53:07.660
You should be able
to get both of these.

00:53:07.660 --> 00:53:11.740
Google and Drive, we've always
invested heavily on the left.

00:53:11.740 --> 00:53:12.660
We're known for that.

00:53:12.660 --> 00:53:13.990
You know us for that.

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:17.020
We are now taking very seriously
the issues on the right.

00:53:17.020 --> 00:53:19.340
This is not a
recent development.

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:21.490
It's been three
years in the making--

00:53:21.490 --> 00:53:24.490
hundreds of engineering
years of effort

00:53:24.490 --> 00:53:26.050
that have gone into this.

00:53:26.050 --> 00:53:33.000
So that's where I should leave
it today, but one more thing.

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:36.600
The one more thing is
innovation, like I said,

00:53:36.600 --> 00:53:37.890
is in our DNA.

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:40.260
And while we are
investing heavily

00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:42.870
in the right-hand side of
that chart, we're Google,

00:53:42.870 --> 00:53:43.560
we're Drive.

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:45.030
That is not all
that we're doing.

00:53:45.030 --> 00:53:46.860
And I want to give
you a preview of where

00:53:46.860 --> 00:53:49.190
Drive is going in the future.

00:53:49.190 --> 00:53:50.820
It's really exciting.

00:53:50.820 --> 00:53:55.620
It's what I personally find very
compelling about this project.

00:53:55.620 --> 00:53:59.670
If you remember Prabhakar
Raghavan's keynote speech

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.410
this morning, he
announced that we

00:54:01.410 --> 00:54:06.090
have 800 million monthly
active users of Drive.

00:54:06.090 --> 00:54:07.890
That's not 800 million
people who have ever

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:09.720
signed up for an account.

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:12.540
We have 800 million people
who use it every month.

00:54:12.540 --> 00:54:15.420
Those 800 million
people have stored

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:18.780
trillions of files in Drive.

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:21.570
And what gets me motivated
to work on this effort

00:54:21.570 --> 00:54:24.840
is to think about what those
trillions of files represent.

00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:27.840
They're not just documents that
have been stored somewhere.

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:30.680
What those files are, really in
the context of an enterprise,

00:54:30.680 --> 00:54:32.640
they're the
enterprise's knowledge.

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:35.520
If I take the example of Google,
we're heavy duty users of this,

00:54:35.520 --> 00:54:36.500
obviously.

00:54:36.500 --> 00:54:38.490
It's every requirements
document that's ever

00:54:38.490 --> 00:54:39.900
been written at the company.

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:41.620
It's every strategic analysis.

00:54:41.620 --> 00:54:42.930
It's every business plan.

00:54:42.930 --> 00:54:45.240
It's every post-mortem
for an outage.

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:47.297
All of these things
are stored in Drive.

00:54:47.297 --> 00:54:49.380
And all of those things,
for an equivalent company

00:54:49.380 --> 00:54:51.754
who might be on-prem, they're
all stored in their filers.

00:54:51.754 --> 00:54:55.140
But is that filer or is that
enterprise content management

00:54:55.140 --> 00:54:59.640
system doing anything to deliver
that content to the right users

00:54:59.640 --> 00:55:01.500
at the right time
to help them be

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:03.930
more productive in their job?

00:55:03.930 --> 00:55:04.610
No.

00:55:04.610 --> 00:55:08.380
When was the last time
your H: popped up and said,

00:55:08.380 --> 00:55:11.340
here's the thing you
need to know right now.

00:55:11.340 --> 00:55:12.360
It's never happened.

00:55:12.360 --> 00:55:15.270
And that's what I find really
compelling about this project.

00:55:15.270 --> 00:55:19.740
So Drive's mission going
forward is to deliver knowledge,

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:22.290
to extract that knowledge
that's stored in the system,

00:55:22.290 --> 00:55:25.080
and deliver it to the right
people within the organization

00:55:25.080 --> 00:55:26.970
at the right time, in
particular, knowledge

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:29.590
workers who need
access to this content.

00:55:29.590 --> 00:55:31.920
Now this might sound familiar.

00:55:31.920 --> 00:55:34.320
If you've been around the
block a couple of times,

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:36.140
you probably remember--

00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:37.830
it was 15 years ago--

00:55:37.830 --> 00:55:40.860
all the enterprise
knowledge management systems

00:55:40.860 --> 00:55:43.600
that were hot at the time.

00:55:43.600 --> 00:55:45.440
And then over the
next 10 years it

00:55:45.440 --> 00:55:47.790
became enterprise content
management systems.

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:51.450
One term gave way to
the other as people

00:55:51.450 --> 00:55:53.910
started to realize these
solutions weren't delivering

00:55:53.910 --> 00:55:55.020
on their promise.

00:55:55.020 --> 00:55:57.960
The problem with enterprise
content management

00:55:57.960 --> 00:56:01.710
today is that everything
in there is stale.

00:56:01.710 --> 00:56:05.670
I've never heard anyone
say, my ECM system--

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:07.530
I find everything
I'm looking for.

00:56:07.530 --> 00:56:09.210
First time, every time.

00:56:09.210 --> 00:56:12.370
It's exactly what I
need to get my job done.

00:56:12.370 --> 00:56:13.830
And the reason the
content there is

00:56:13.830 --> 00:56:19.050
stale is that they require
people to go in and manually

00:56:19.050 --> 00:56:20.430
add metadata tags.

00:56:20.430 --> 00:56:22.320
This was a requirements
document and this

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:26.120
was on a project about
lighter-than-air flight

00:56:26.120 --> 00:56:27.536
or whatever it was.

00:56:27.536 --> 00:56:28.410
People don't do that.

00:56:28.410 --> 00:56:29.910
It's like flossing.

00:56:29.910 --> 00:56:31.890
Your dentist tells you
should floss every day.

00:56:31.890 --> 00:56:33.000
You probably don't.

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:36.240
That's exactly how enterprise
content management systems are.

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:40.630
And, as Google, we have the
ability to solve that problem.

00:56:40.630 --> 00:56:44.370
Today, we're announcing
Quick Access for Team Drives.

00:56:44.370 --> 00:56:47.550
This is, really, a
revolution in Google Drive.

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:50.120
It might look very simple
and the user interface

00:56:50.120 --> 00:56:53.490
is deceptively simple
on the surface.

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:56.280
It's four to 10 documents
that you probably

00:56:56.280 --> 00:56:59.400
want to look at right now
in a sliding carousel,

00:56:59.400 --> 00:57:01.060
but that's just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:57:01.060 --> 00:57:03.570
What you don't realize, looking
at this very simple user

00:57:03.570 --> 00:57:06.930
interface, is the
massive amount of machine

00:57:06.930 --> 00:57:09.340
learning under the hood
that's powering this.

00:57:09.340 --> 00:57:12.510
And what it's doing is it's
looking across your team.

00:57:12.510 --> 00:57:15.287
What document has your
boss just commented on?

00:57:15.287 --> 00:57:17.370
What are the trending
topics in your organization?

00:57:17.370 --> 00:57:19.370
What meetings do you have
coming up later today?

00:57:19.370 --> 00:57:21.270
And given all of
that information,

00:57:21.270 --> 00:57:24.210
the machine learning model
processes what do you really

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:25.980
need to know right now?

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:27.510
No tagging required.

00:57:27.510 --> 00:57:32.550
And it serves up that knowledge
to you at the right time

00:57:32.550 --> 00:57:34.230
without anyone in
your organization

00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:37.380
having to manually tag anything.

00:57:37.380 --> 00:57:40.860
If you think about 800
million users and trillions

00:57:40.860 --> 00:57:44.130
of documents stored in
Drive, this is the only way

00:57:44.130 --> 00:57:45.220
to solve that problem.

00:57:45.220 --> 00:57:49.020
There's no way individuals
could go in and tag and curate

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:50.220
that amount of data.

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:52.805
We really need the Google
machine learning capabilities

00:57:52.805 --> 00:57:54.930
that you've heard so much
about at this conference.

00:57:54.930 --> 00:57:58.252
We run custom silicon for
optimizing our machine

00:57:58.252 --> 00:57:59.460
learning models, for example.

00:57:59.460 --> 00:58:01.384
We're years ahead of
competitors there.

00:58:01.384 --> 00:58:02.550
That's what's powering this.

00:58:02.550 --> 00:58:04.560
And you can imagine where
this goes from here.

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:07.059
Not just delivering the document
you need at the right time,

00:58:07.059 --> 00:58:09.619
but extracting the factual
content from those documents

00:58:09.619 --> 00:58:11.160
and delivering it
to you, helping you

00:58:11.160 --> 00:58:13.470
identify the experts
within your organization.

00:58:13.470 --> 00:58:15.720
So if you're thinking about
migrating onto Drive, what

00:58:15.720 --> 00:58:18.660
you need to know is you're going
to take all that content out

00:58:18.660 --> 00:58:20.730
of your legacy content
management system.

00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:22.350
You're going to put
it up into Drive

00:58:22.350 --> 00:58:26.092
and that content will, over time
and continually, get unlocked

00:58:26.092 --> 00:58:28.050
and the value is going
to get delivered to you.

00:58:28.050 --> 00:58:29.040
Couple hard stats.

00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:33.070
So Quick Access has been in
production for a set of users

00:58:33.070 --> 00:58:34.300
now for a period of time.

00:58:34.300 --> 00:58:37.180
It's one of the most impactful
projects of this sort

00:58:37.180 --> 00:58:38.980
that I've ever seen at Google.

00:58:38.980 --> 00:58:42.250
It's 52% faster than users
having to go and find

00:58:42.250 --> 00:58:43.300
their content manually.

00:58:43.300 --> 00:58:46.690
We can measure this empirically
in a controlled way.

00:58:46.690 --> 00:58:50.890
It is already being
used to open 40%

00:58:50.890 --> 00:58:52.120
of all the content in Drive.

00:58:52.120 --> 00:58:53.950
For the users who
are using this,

00:58:53.950 --> 00:58:56.320
almost half the
time Quick Access

00:58:56.320 --> 00:58:57.940
delivers them right
what they need

00:58:57.940 --> 00:58:59.439
and they don't have
to go find this.

00:58:59.439 --> 00:59:01.150
This is not something
that's only getting

00:59:01.150 --> 00:59:03.550
used out in the margins.

00:59:03.550 --> 00:59:06.670
It's really, now, the
fundamental way people

00:59:06.670 --> 00:59:08.200
find content in Drive.

00:59:08.200 --> 00:59:11.860
And for the set of users who
have been using Quick Access so

00:59:11.860 --> 00:59:16.210
far, every day it saves them
5 and 1/2 weeks of busy work,

00:59:16.210 --> 00:59:18.374
digging around
looking for content.

00:59:18.374 --> 00:59:20.540
So that's what you can
expect from us in the future.

00:59:20.540 --> 00:59:23.410
That's why I'm really
excited to work on this.

00:59:23.410 --> 00:59:26.170
And that all builds on this
enterprise-grade foundation

00:59:26.170 --> 00:59:28.840
you just heard us
spend so much time on.

00:59:28.840 --> 00:59:30.366
We know that you
wouldn't get access

00:59:30.366 --> 00:59:31.990
to these kind of
capabilities, wouldn't

00:59:31.990 --> 00:59:33.700
be able to access
them, without having

00:59:33.700 --> 00:59:36.530
that enterprise-grade
foundation under the hood.

00:59:36.530 --> 00:59:40.540
So three to-do's for the end
of the presentation here.

00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:43.810
If you're a G Suite
customer, consider

00:59:43.810 --> 00:59:45.926
rolling out Team Drives
to your organization.

00:59:45.926 --> 00:59:47.800
It's under your control
when you roll it out.

00:59:47.800 --> 00:59:50.600
There's a link there you
can follow with information.

00:59:50.600 --> 00:59:52.990
Two, please sign up,
if you're interested,

00:59:52.990 --> 00:59:55.840
in the Drive File Stream
Early Adopter Program.

00:59:55.840 --> 00:59:57.340
That's if you're a
G Suite customer.

00:59:57.340 --> 00:59:59.950
If you're not G Suite
customer, please consider us

00:59:59.950 --> 01:00:01.030
